{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on small probability outcomes (less than 20%) and adjusts their weight by increasing the probability by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, the 0 Euro outcome has a 10% probability (below 20%), so its weighted probability becomes 20%. The -3000 Euro outcome has a 90% probability, which remains unchanged. In Lottery B, the 0 Euro outcome has a 55% probability (unchanged), and the -6000 Euro outcome has 45% (also unchanged). Only the 10% outcome in Lottery A qualifies for probability weighting. The adjusted expected value for small-probability outcomes is: Lottery A: (0.10 + 0.10) × 0 = 0; Lottery B: no small-probability outcome (both ≥ 20%), so adjustment not applied. Thus, the reason evaluates Lottery A’s small-probability outcome with increased weight but finds no such outcome in B. The reason prefers the lottery with the more favorable (less negative or more positive) weighted sum of small-probability outcomes. Here, only A has a modified outcome, but it is 0, so no gain. However, the rule focuses only on small-probability outcomes, not the full lottery.",
